<Subpage
  title="Autocorrect on Windows 11 and 10: turn it on for every app"
  description="Windows has autocorrect switches, but they reach only some apps. Here is what is built in, where it stops, and how to get phone-style autocorrect in every app."
  crumb="Autocorrect on Windows"
>
  <h1>How to get phone-style autocorrect on Windows 11</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    On a phone, a typo fixes itself as you type. On Windows, most apps underline it in red and wait for you. Here is what
    Windows offers, where it stops, and how to get autocorrect in every app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="does-windows-have-autocorrect">Does Windows have autocorrect?</h2>
  <p>
    Partly. Windows 10 and 11 have an <strong>Autocorrect misspelled words</strong> switch, but it only works in apps that use
    Windows' own text services. Most chat apps, browsers and code editors don't, so in those it does nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-get-autocorrect-on-pc">How do I get autocorrect on my PC?</h2>
  <p>
    Turn on the Windows switch first (steps below), since it is free and built in. For autocorrect in every app, install a
    system-wide tool such as <a href="/download/">unflub</a>, which fixes a typo the moment you finish the word, anywhere you
    type. If the Windows switch seems to do nothing, <a href="/autocorrect-not-working/">here is why</a>.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" aria-label="Example: unflub fixes teh to the as soon as the word ends.">
    <p class="line loose" aria-hidden="true">See you at <span class="fx lit">the<Pill from="teh" to="the" /></span> station</p>
  </figure>

  <h2>Turn on autocorrect in Windows 11</h2>
  <p>Windows 11 and Windows 10 have two switches for this:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <span class="path">Settings</span> and go to <span class="path">Time &amp; language</span>, then <span class="path">Typing</span>.</li>
    <li>Turn on <strong>Autocorrect misspelled words</strong>.</li>
    <li>Turn on <strong>Highlight misspelled words</strong> if you also want red underlines.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    These switches were designed with the touch keyboard in mind. With a physical keyboard they only reach apps that use
    Windows' own text services, so many browsers, chat apps and code editors are left out. There is little to tune: Windows
    keeps a dictionary of words you add, but you can't skip an app, set how eager it is, or expand shortcuts.
  </p>

  <h2>Autocorrect inside a single app</h2>
  <p>Some apps have their own autocorrect, which works only inside that app:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Word and Outlook</strong> fix common typos through AutoCorrect, under
      <span class="path">File</span>, <span class="path">Options</span>, <span class="path">Proofing</span>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Browsers</strong> such as Chrome, Edge and Firefox underline misspellings in text boxes. You right-click a word to
      pick a fix; nothing is changed as you type.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>That covers documents and some web forms, but not chat apps, desktop tools or prompt boxes, and each one behaves differently.</p>

  <h2>Autocorrect in every app, with unflub</h2>
  <p>
    unflub is a small, free app that brings phone-style autocorrect to every text field on Windows. It fixes a typo the moment
    you finish the word, in any app. Corrections work fully offline (updates and anonymous stats need a connection).
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="/download/">Download unflub</a> and run the installer.</li>
    <li>It starts in the system tray. Type anywhere: chat, email, the browser, a prompt box.</li>
    <li>When a word is fixed, a small capsule shows what changed. Press Backspace right away to undo it, and unflub remembers that word.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    That includes the apps people most often ask about: Discord, Microsoft Teams, Slack, and AI chat boxes such as ChatGPT and
    Claude in the browser or their desktop apps.
  </p>
  <p>A few things make it behave like a phone keyboard rather than a blunt find-and-replace:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It leaves code alone: camelCase, snake_case, file paths, links and anything in backticks are never touched.</li>
    <li>It skips password fields, terminals and password managers.</li>
    <li>It only fixes a word when it is confident, and you can set how confident it must be.</li>
    <li>You can add your own words, and shortcuts like <span class="path">omw</span> to <em>on my way</em>.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Free and open source.</strong> unflub is MIT licensed, with no account and no paid tier. The code is
      on <a href={SITE.repo}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Subpage>

